**[VS Code – create `test.py` in the main directory]**

```python
from selenium import webdriver
from selenium.webdriver.common.by import By
from selenium.webdriver.chrome.options import Options
import time

options = Options()

# Connect to the Selenium browser container
driver = webdriver.Remote(
    command_executor="http://selenium:4444",
    options=options
)

try:

    # Open the application using its Docker network name
    driver.get("http://my-web-app")

    # Enter registration details
    driver.find_element(By.ID, "firstName").send_keys("Lahari")

    driver.find_element(By.ID, "lastName").send_keys("K")

    driver.find_element(By.ID, "email").send_keys("lahari@example.com")

    driver.find_element(By.ID, "phNo").send_keys("9876543210")

    # Submit the form
    driver.find_element(By.ID, "submitBtn").click()

    time.sleep(2)

    # Verify the success message
    message = driver.find_element(By.ID, "successmsg").text

    print("Success Message:", message)

    if message == "Thank You For Registering :)":
        print("Test Passed")
    else:
        print("Test Failed")

finally:

    driver.quit()
```

### Step 11: Create the test runner Dockerfile

**[VS Code – create `Dockerfile.test` in the main directory]**

```dockerfile
# Use Python as the base image
FROM python:3.12-slim

# Set the working directory
WORKDIR /tests

# Install Selenium
RUN pip install selenium

# Copy the test script
COPY test.py .

# Execute the test
CMD ["python", "test.py"]
```

### Step 12: Build the test runner image

**[PowerShell – in the main directory]**

```powershell
docker build -f Dockerfile.test -t selenium-test .
```

### Step 13: Wait for Selenium to become ready

